WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.722
The OT threat landscape includes cybercriminals, ransomware operators, state-linked groups, insiders, vendors, hacktivists, negligent users, misconfiguration, and accidents.

00:00:09.722 --> 00:00:18.946
Their methods and intentions differ, but risk emerges where access and system weakness meet an operational consequence.

00:00:18.946 --> 00:00:22.187
OT consequences extend beyond data.

00:00:22.187 --> 00:00:29.915
Incidents can affect safety, uptime, process integrity, equipment, environmental conditions, production, and public services.

00:00:29.915 --> 00:00:39.139
Long lifecycles, legacy technology, flat networks, remote support, and expensive downtime can increase exposure or constrain response.

00:00:39.139 --> 00:00:43.876
Effective defense begins with asset and connection visibility.

00:00:43.876 --> 00:00:58.085
Segment enterprise and control functions, manage remote access, protect engineering systems, monitor meaningful boundaries, maintain recoverable configurations, govern change, and prepare incident decisions with operators and engineers.

00:00:58.085 --> 00:01:03.320
Advanced threats deserve attention, but routine weaknesses remain important.

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:13.542
Shared credentials, incorrect rules, unsupported systems, unreviewed changes, and weak third-party governance can create serious incidents without specialized malware.

00:01:13.542 --> 00:01:18.278
Controls should reduce several threat paths at once.

00:01:18.278 --> 00:01:22.516
The strongest perspective is operational and evidence-based.

00:01:22.516 --> 00:01:36.726
Ask what exists, who can reach it, what they can influence, how abnormal activity would be detected, what safeguards remain, and how trusted operation would be restored.

00:01:36.726 --> 00:01:44.952
OT threat management is continuous collaboration among cybersecurity, engineering, operations, vendors, and accountable risk owners.
